    Execute the whole graph against a stub model
    
    The orchestration is the part that must be correct regardless of what any model
    says, and until now it could only be verified against a live provider -- which
    this account's rate limit made unreliable. These tests run the real Runner, the
    real graph, the real routing nodes and the real tester agent, replacing only the
    model and the sandbox. No network, no quota.
    
    Eleven paths are covered end to end, including the ones that matter most:
    
    - A green suite on a real diff reaches the report; the tester's numbers come
      from the sandbox rather than from a model.
    - Never-passing tests exhaust the budget instead of looping, and an exhausted
      run still terminates at the report.
    - A green suite with no diff never reaches the reviewer at all -- the regression
      the previous commit fixed, now pinned at the graph level.
    - A sandbox failure never reaches the reviewer.
    - CHANGES_REQUIRED returns to the engineer; a major finding returns to the
      architect.
    - The iteration budget is spent exactly, verified by counting sandbox calls.
    
    Writing the stub surfaced two things worth recording in the test itself: ADK
    asks for structured output two different ways depending on whether the agent
    also holds tools, and it validates that output against the schema before it
    reaches state -- so a nonsense verdict fails the node rather than becoming an
    approval.
    
    249 tests pass.
    
