    Bound the HTTP server's authority: authentication and path confinement
    
    A security review of the previous commit was right: POST /api/run started an
    agent that writes files and executes commands, took an arbitrary host path as
    the target directory, and required no authentication. Exposed on any interface,
    that is remote code execution.
    
    Two controls, neither of which defaults to open:
    
    - SE_AGENT_API_TOKEN, compared with hmac.compare_digest so the check cannot leak
      a prefix by timing. With no token configured the server serves loopback only,
      because starting it on 0.0.0.0 would otherwise silently hand code execution to
      the network.
    - SE_AGENT_REPO_ROOT confines the target directory. Paths are resolved before
      the containment check, so traversal and symlinks both fail. Without the
      variable the repo field is refused outright rather than defaulting to
      anywhere on disk.
    
    Both are resolved before the stream opens, so a rejection is an ordinary
    401/403 rather than an error buried inside a 200 body. The run is now attributed
    to the caller's identity instead of a hardcoded user id.
    
    238 tests pass.
    
